<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calc-desk.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calc-desk-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calc-desk.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Plain-English Property Claim Glossary</h1><p class="lede">41 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, from actual cash value to wind-driven rain, defined without jargon.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="gfilter"><label for="gloss-q">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-q" type="search" placeholder="Try “ice dam” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gcount>41 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Jump to letter"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-V">V</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value</dt><dd>The value of damaged property after depreciation is subtracted from its replacement cost. Many policies pay this amount first and the rest once repairs are complete.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expense</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra costs of living elsewhere while your home is unlivable after a covered loss, such as rent, meals and mileage above your normal spending.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision of an appraisal panel stating the amount of loss. Under typical wording it is binding on amount once signed by any two of the three panel members.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party ask for a panel to settle a disagreement over the amount of a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-B"><dt>Bureau of Insurance</dt><dd>The Maine state agency, within the Department of Professional and Financial Regulation, that regulates insurers and takes consumer complaints about claim handling.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-C"><dt>Cedar shingle</dt><dd>A wood roofing or siding material common on older Maine capes and coastal cottages. Repair estimates often turn on matching weathered shingles and the higher labor involved.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that covers the dwelling itself, including attached structures like a connected garage or ell.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that covers other structures on the property not attached to the house, such as a detached barn, shed or fence.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that covers personal property, meaning the contents you own such as furniture, clothing and tools.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage D</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that covers loss of use, usually additional living expense or lost rental income while the home cannot be lived in.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-D"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, the property, coverage limits, deductibles and the policy period.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss you pay before the policy pays. It is usually a flat dollar amount but can be a percentage for some perils.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and condition, subtracted from replacement cost to arrive at actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial stake in the outcome of the claim beyond an agreed fee and no relationship that would bias the result. Most appraisal clauses require it.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-E"><dt>Ell</dt><dd>A wing attached to a Maine farmhouse, often part of the connected big house, little house, back house and barn layout. Damage to an ell is usually covered as part of the dwelling.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-F"><dt>Freeze exclusion</dt><dd>A policy provision denying coverage for frozen pipe damage unless the owner kept heat on or drained the plumbing and shut off the water. Also called a heat-maintenance condition.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-H"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>The depreciation a carrier withholds from an initial payment until repairs or replacement are completed, under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-I"><dt>Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-sealing waterproof membrane installed under roofing along eaves and valleys to stop water backed up by ice dams. Building codes in cold climates often require it at the eaves.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice that forms at the roof edge when melting snow refreezes, causing water to back up under shingles and into ceilings and walls.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in a repair estimate that describes one task or material, with a quantity, unit and price.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs look uniform, for example when a discontinued siding color cannot be matched.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>The steps taken right after a loss to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof, extracting water or running dehumidifiers. Most policies require reasonable mitigation.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-N"><dt>Named storm deductible</dt><dd>A separate deductible that applies when damage comes from a named storm or hurricane, often set as a percentage of the dwelling limit. Also called a percentage deductible.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-O"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for the added cost of bringing repaired parts of a building up to current building codes, usually limited to a percentage of the dwelling limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit</dt><dd>The general contractor’s charge for coordinating trades and running the job, commonly figured as a percentage added to an estimate when several trades are needed.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-P"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed that many policies require within a set time after the carrier asks for it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Puffback</dt><dd>A misfire in an oil burner that blows soot and smoke through the furnace, ductwork and living space. Cleanup can reach every surface in the house.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation that a replacement cost policy pays back once repairs or replacement are completed and documented.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Replacement cost value</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without subtracting depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the repairs needed for each, which forms the basis of an estimate.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Square</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface. Estimates count roofing in squares, plus a waste allowance.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Standing-seam metal roof</dt><dd>A metal roof with raised interlocking seams, common in Maine snow country because it sheds snow well. Repairs often involve whole panels rather than small patches.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An added labor charge for working on roofs above a certain pitch, reflecting the extra safety gear and time steep slopes demand.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Storm surge</dt><dd>Coastal flooding pushed ashore by a storm’s winds. It is treated as flood and excluded under standard homeowners policies.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment after the first estimate, usually because hidden damage was found or items were missed.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Surplus lines carrier</dt><dd>An insurer not licensed as a standard admitted carrier in the state but allowed to write coverage that the standard market will not. Many hard-to-place Maine homes are insured this way.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel, chosen by the two appraisers, who decides items they cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Unoccupancy</dt><dd>A condition where a building is furnished but nobody is living in it, such as a seasonal camp in winter. Some policies limit certain coverages after a set period of unoccupancy.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-V"><dt>Vacancy clause</dt><dd>A policy provision that limits or removes coverage for some perils when a building has been empty of people and most belongings for a stated period, often 30 or 60 days.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-W"><dt>Wind-driven rain</dt><dd>Rain forced into a building by high winds. Policies often cover it only when wind first creates an opening in the roof or walls.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurance carriers and adjusters to price repairs line by line using regional price lists.</dd></div></dl><p class="gnone" hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article>
